121":"child","H2205":"elder","H3478":"Israel","H559":"to say","H413":"to(wards)","H3947":"take","H5695":"calf","H1241":"cattle","H2403":"sin","H352":"ram","H5930":"burnt offering","H8549":"unblemished","H7126":"come","H6440":"before","H3068":"LORD","H1696":"speak","H8163":"hairy","H5795":"goat","H3532":"lamb","H8141":"year","H7794":"cattle","H8002":"peace offering","H2076":"to sacrifice","H4503":"offering","H1101":"to mix","H8081":"oil","H3588":"for","H7200":"see","H853":"[Obj.]","H834":"which","H6680":"to command","H168":"tent","H4150":"time appointed","H3605":"all","H5712":"congregation","H5975":"stand","H2088":"this","H1697":"word","H6213":"do","H3519":"glory","H4196":"altar","H3722":"to atone","H1157":"about/through/for","H5971":"people","H7133":"offering","H7819":"to slaughter","H1818":"blood","H2881":"to dip","H676":"finger","H5414":"give","H5921":"upon","H7161":"horn","H3332":"pour","H3247":"(Gate of the) Foundation","H2459":"fat","H3629":"kidney","H3508":"lobe","H4480":"from","H3516":"liver","H6999":"burn","H1320":"flesh","H5785":"skin","H8313":"to burn","H784":"fire","H2351":"outside","H4264":"camp","H4672":"to find","H2236":"to scatter","H5439":"around","H5409":"piece","H7218":"head","H7364":"wash","H7130":"among","H3767":"leg","H2398":"to sin","H7223":"first","H4941":"judgement","H4390":"to fill","H3709":"palm","H905":"pole","H1242":"morning","H2077":"sacrifice","H451":"fat tail","H4374":"covering","H7760":"make","H2373":"breast","H7785":"leg","H3225":"right","H5130":"to sprinkle","H8573":"wave offering","H5375":"raise","H3027":"hand","H1288":"to bless","H3381":"to go down","H935":"come","H3318":"come","H398":"to eat","H7442":"to overcome","H5307":"fall"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Come. From the consecration of the tabernacle, (Menochius) and of Aaron. — Israel. The princes of the tribes. (Calmet) — They were to offer sacrifice by the hands of their new priests."],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Calf. As they had formerly adored a calf, so now they sacrifice one to God. (St. Jerome in Jer. vii.)"],["haydock",3,null,"haydock","Children. Samaritan and Septuagint, “the ancients,” or princes of the people, for whom a he-goat is sacrificed. — Old. Not above, though they might be younger."],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Offering, &c. Hebrew simply, “and a flour-offering tempered with oil; for,” &c. (Haydock) — All these sacrifices were accompanied with an offering of this nature, as they were in imitation of a dinner presented to God. (Menochius) — You. By the cloud, resting upon the tabernacle, or by fire proceeding thence. God will manifest his presence by miracles, ver. 24."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Thy sin. Christ needed not daily (as the other priests) to offer sacrifices, first for his own sins, and then for the people’s, Hebrews vii. 27."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","The altar of holocausts; as he is yet considered only as a private person; afterwards he touches the altar of perfumes. (Calmet)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Burnt, or placed in order to be burnt by the fire sent by God, ver. 24. (Menochius)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Camp. According to the prescriptions given, chap. iv. 12."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Water. Hebrew adds, “he burnt them upon the holocaust, upon the altar.”"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","And expiating the altar. Hebrew, “he offered it (the goat) for sin, as the first,” for himself, placing the parts of the victim upon his own holocaust. (Haydock) — The Chaldean says, “he expiated the altar with the blood of the he-goat, as he did before.”"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Holocaust. Which were religiously observed every day. The law respecting the libations was given already, though it be related, Numbers xv. 4. (Menochius)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Elevating them. After which they were used by the priest, chap. vii. 31. (Calmet) — As. Samaritan and some Hebrew manuscripts read, “as the Lord had commanded Moses.” (Kennicott)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Hands. Thus representing the form of a cross, on which Christ redeemed us; in memory of which we still make the same sign. (Worthington) — Them. The blessing is recorded, Numbers vi. 24. And the Lord bless thee, &c. (Menochius) — In blessing an individual, the priest laid his hands upon him; but he stretched them out towards the multitude, as a mark of superiority."],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","Testimony. To offer incense, which always preceded the morning holocaust. — Glory; or fire, probably issuing from the tabernacle, and consuming the victims in a moment. Thus God was pleased to shew his approbation of the priests and victims, (Calmet) and at the same time, to impress a religious awe upon the minds of the spectators. (Haydock) — This fire was carefully preserved and nourished by the priests with wood; though the Rabbins say, this was done only to conceal the miracle of its perpetual continuance. A fire, of the same nature, came down upon the victims, when Solomon dedicated his temple, (2 Paralipomenon vii. 1,) and was kept burning till the captivity, when it was hidden in a cistern. Being found afterwards, like a muddy water, God kindled it again, (2 Machabees i. 18; ii. 10,) and it was not lost till the persecution of Epiphanes."],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","The Lord: 2 Machabees ii. 10, explains this text. Fire came down from heaven, appearing like a flash of lightning, in the midst of the victims. (Josephus, Antiquities iii. 9.) (Calmet)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}